From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 14:58:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: Carry the response body in the response instead of a
 done channel

A response body was held twice: as a state in a shared
`Arc<Mutex<ResponseBody>>`, and as a `done_chan` that every fetch function
passed along and had to reset whenever it replaced the response. Keeping
the two paired by convention went wrong in several ways:

- A fetch cancelled right after its headers returned a network error while
  `done_chan` still held a sender, so `wait_for_response` waited forever
  and the fetch never ended.
- A response replaced by a network error, after a failed CORS check or a
  CORP, CSP, nosniff, MIME type or mixed content block, kept its channel,
  so its body was still delivered to the consumer.
- Step 22 of main fetch (null body status, HEAD) blanked the state, but not
  the chunks already queued in the channel.
- A navigation through a `web+` protocol handler returned a response whose
  body an inner fetch had already handed to `DiscardFetch`.
- Producers had to know whether a consumer inside net (subresource
  integrity, preloads) needed the whole body, and `file:` and `blob:`
  bodies were kept whole in net in any case. An in-memory `blob:` body was
  never kept, so its integrity check always failed.

`ResponseBody` is now `Null`, `Bytes`, or a `Stream` of the body stream
type added in the previous patch, and the response owns it.
`wait_for_response` is the one place that hands a body over, and it keeps
the body as `Bytes` for preloads and for subresource integrity. A body whose
producer goes away without finishing ends as failed instead of hanging. The
body of a redirect, or of a response replaced after a 401 or 407, is
discarded instead of being queued while the next request runs; readers of
files and of the cache stop when nobody reads their body.
`ProtocolHandler::load` loses its `done_chan` parameter, and
`servo::protocol_handler` exports the body stream. `Response` is no longer
`Clone`: `try_clone` copies a response whose body is not a stream. The page
content protocol handler runs main fetch recursively, so the outer fetch
hands the handler URL's body over.

Otherwise behaviour does not change: a body cut short by the connection
still ends as aborted with a successful end of file, a discarded network
body is still read to its end so that its connection can be reused and the
cache can store it, and subresource integrity still forwards the body
before checking it. One exception: a preload whose body is cut short is
now kept without a body instead of with the bytes that arrived; both are
wrong, and a later patch makes it a network error.

Validation: `./mach test-unit -p servo-net`: 490 tests pass, 7 of them new. On ohos-main 67173a4bf2b, `test_fetch_cancelled_while_its_headers_are_held_ends` fails with "the fetch did not end", `test_body_of_a_response_that_fails_the_cors_check_is_not_delivered` with all 19 body bytes delivered, `test_body_of_a_response_blocked_by_nosniff_is_not_delivered` with all 12, and `test_navigation_through_a_page_content_protocol_handler_delivers_the_body` with none. Negative controls on this patch: without applying step 22's null body, `test_body_of_a_null_body_status_is_not_delivered` gets 7 bytes; with a test producer that keeps its sender alive, `test_body_whose_producer_stops_without_finishing_ends_the_fetch` does not end; without keeping preloaded bodies, `test_fetch_of_a_preload_keeps_its_body` finds no body. `./mach check`, `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings` and `./mach test-tidy` pass.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote this patch and its tests following docs/fetch-body-stream-plan-2026-10-22.md in the workspace, found the cancellation hang, the delivery of blocked bodies and the protocol handler regression, and applied an independent review's findings)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
